# Performance Optimization Standards

You are a performance-conscious engineer. Fast apps win users. Slow apps lose them. Every millisecond matters.

## Core Web Vitals — Targets

| Metric | Target | What It Measures |
|--------|--------|-----------------|
| **LCP** (Largest Contentful Paint) | < 2.5s | How fast main content loads |
| **INP** (Interaction to Next Paint) | < 200ms | How fast the page responds to clicks |
| **CLS** (Cumulative Layout Shift) | < 0.1 | How stable the layout is |

## Frontend — Next.js Performance

### Image Optimization

```tsx
// ✅ Always use next/image — automatic WebP, lazy loading, responsive
import Image from 'next/image';

<Image
  src="/hero.jpg"
  alt="Hero banner"
  width={1200}
  height={600}
  priority              // Only for above-the-fold images (LCP candidates)
  placeholder="blur"    // Smooth loading experience
  blurDataURL={blurUrl} // Base64 blur placeholder
  sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 50vw, 33vw"
/>

// ❌ Never use raw <img> tags
// ❌ Never load full-size images for thumbnails
// ❌ Never skip alt attributes
```

### Code Splitting & Lazy Loading

```tsx
// ✅ Dynamic imports for heavy components
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

const HeavyChart = dynamic(() => import('@/components/Chart'), {
  loading: () => <ChartSkeleton />,
  ssr: false, // Skip SSR for client-only components
});

const MarkdownEditor = dynamic(() => import('@/components/MarkdownEditor'), {
  loading: () => <EditorSkeleton />,
});
```

### Font Optimization

```tsx
// ✅ Use next/font — automatic self-hosting, zero layout shift
import { Inter } from 'next/font/google';

const inter = Inter({
  subsets: ['latin'],
  display: 'swap',        // Prevent FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text)
  variable: '--font-inter',
});

// In layout.tsx
<html className={inter.variable}>
```

### React Performance Patterns

```tsx
// ✅ Memoize expensive computations
import { useMemo, useCallback, memo } from 'react';

// Memoize expensive calculations
const sortedItems = useMemo(() =>
  items.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
  [items]
);

// Memoize callbacks passed to children
const handleDelete = useCallback((id: string) => {
  deleteItem(id);
}, [deleteItem]);

// Memoize child components that receive object/array props
const UserCard = memo(function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});

// ✅ Use React.startTransition for non-urgent updates
import { useTransition } from 'react';

function SearchPage() {
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  function handleSearch(value: string) {
    setQuery(value);                    // Urgent: update input immediately
    startTransition(() => {
      setResults(filterItems(value));   // Non-urgent: can wait
    });
  }
}
```

**When to NOT memoize:**
- Simple components with primitive props (string, number, boolean).
- Components that always re-render anyway (parent always re-renders).
- Don't prematurely optimize — measure first with React DevTools Profiler.

### Skeleton Loading (Not Spinners)

```tsx
// ✅ Skeletons preserve layout and feel faster than spinners
function UserCardSkeleton() {
  return (
    <div className="animate-pulse">
      <div className="h-12 w-12 rounded-full bg-gray-200" />
      <div className="h-4 w-3/4 bg-gray-200 rounded mt-2" />
      <div className="h-3 w-1/2 bg-gray-200 rounded mt-1" />
    </div>
  );
}

// Use in loading.tsx for route-level loading
export default function Loading() {
  return <UserCardSkeleton />;
}
```

## Backend — API Performance

### Caching Strategy

```typescript
// ✅ Cache-Control headers for static/semi-static data
// Immutable assets (hashed filenames)
'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=31536000, immutable'

// API responses (short cache + revalidation)
'Cache-Control': 'public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300'

// User-specific / sensitive data
'Cache-Control': 'private, no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate'
```

```typescript
// ✅ Next.js ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration)
// For pages that change infrequently
export const revalidate = 3600; // Revalidate every hour

// ✅ On-demand revalidation for content updates
import { revalidatePath, revalidateTag } from 'next/cache';

// In a Server Action after content update:
revalidatePath('/blog');
revalidateTag('posts');
```

```typescript
// ✅ Redis caching for expensive queries
import { redis } from '@/lib/redis';

async function getPopularPosts(): Promise<Post[]> {
  const cacheKey = 'popular_posts';
  const cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);

  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const posts = await db.post.findMany({
    orderBy: { views: 'desc' },
    take: 10,
  });

  await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(posts), 'EX', 300); // 5 min TTL
  return posts;
}
```

### Response Compression

```typescript
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  compress: true, // gzip/brotli (enabled by default in Next.js)
};
```

```python
# FastAPI
from fastapi.middleware.gzip import GZipMiddleware

app.add_middleware(GZipMiddleware, minimum_size=1000)  # Compress responses > 1KB
```

## Database Performance

### Query Optimization

```typescript
// ❌ N+1 Problem: One query per user's posts
const users = await prisma.user.findMany();
for (const user of users) {
  user.posts = await prisma.post.findMany({ where: { authorId: user.id } });
}

// ✅ Eager loading: One query with includes
const users = await prisma.user.findMany({
  include: { posts: true },
});

// ✅ Select only needed fields
const users = await prisma.user.findMany({
  select: { id: true, name: true, email: true }, // Never select password
});
```

```python
# ✅ SQLAlchemy eager loading
from sqlalchemy.orm import joinedload

stmt = select(User).options(joinedload(User.posts)).limit(20)
result = await db.execute(stmt)
```

### Indexing Rules

```prisma
// schema.prisma — Add indexes for frequently queried columns
model User {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  email     String   @unique
  name      String
  role      String
  status    String
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([status, createdAt])  // Composite index for filtered + sorted queries
  @@index([role])               // Filter by role
}

model Post {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  title     String
  authorId  String
  status    String
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  author    User     @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id])

  @@index([authorId, createdAt]) // "Posts by user, newest first"
  @@index([status])              // "All published posts"
}
```

**Database Rules:**
- Index every column used in `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`, or `JOIN`.
- Use composite indexes for multi-column queries (order matters!).
- Monitor slow queries. Set up query logging for queries > 100ms.
- Use connection pooling (PgBouncer, Prisma connection pool).
- Paginate all list queries. Never `SELECT *` without `LIMIT`.

## Bundle Analysis

```bash
# ✅ Analyze your bundle size regularly
npx @next/bundle-analyzer

# Set budgets
# Main bundle: < 200KB gzipped
# Per-page JS: < 100KB gzipped
# Third-party: < 150KB gzipped
```

**Bundle Rules:**
- Monitor bundle size in CI. Set size budgets and fail on regressions.
- Use tree-shakeable imports: `import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button'`, not `import * as UI from '@/components/ui'`.
- Audit node_modules quarterly. Remove unused packages.
- Use `import()` for large libraries only needed on specific pages.

## Performance Checklist

- [ ] Images optimized (WebP, responsive sizes, lazy loaded)
- [ ] Fonts self-hosted with `display: swap`
- [ ] Heavy components dynamically imported
- [ ] API responses cached appropriately
- [ ] Database queries indexed and paginated
- [ ] Bundle size under budget
- [ ] Core Web Vitals passing (LCP < 2.5s, INP < 200ms, CLS < 0.1)
